RECEIPT MECHANISMS 1.0.1

Repair of the 61aea606e126 run
Version 1.0.0 stopped before any model fit at a combined secondary receipt
check. Its public error did not identify which predicate failed. Version 1.0.1
fixes an extra material restriction absent from the recorded claimed-win arm,
exports field-by-field receipt audits, and separates primary comparisons from
secondary dose validation. This is a code/contract repair; the supplied failed
report cannot establish which predicate triggered on the actual rows.

The exact old frozen receipt cohort is preserved. No mismatched row is deleted
or relabelled. Any remaining receipt, timing, identity or payoff problem blocks
ALL secondary dose fits, while valid primary comparisons finish automatically.
The final status then reads PRIMARY_COMPLETE_SECONDARY_BLOCKED, with exact
counts in receipt_cohort_audit.csv and receipt_arm_patterns.csv.

The original recorded exposure definition is copied with its source checksum
in authority/recorded_receipt_definition.txt. The primary comparisons' group
definitions and statistical specifications remain unchanged.

Purpose
Examine whether later generosity differs after discretionary draws, claimed
disconnection losses, ordinary draws/losses, and explicitly flagged no-material
draws. This is an observational mechanism diagnostic alongside the existing IV.

Required existing Mac project
  /Volumes/XT_Pro/lichess_kindness
Required reviewed runs
  derived/replication/run2_phase25_analysis_v120_PRIVATE/79bd3599a995
  derived/replication/run2_environment_diagnostics_v100_PRIVATE/cea0b052e5f2
Also uses their immutable metadata snapshot and certified 24-month main panels.
Hashes/configuration receipts are checked; no newest-run discovery or fallback
to a different sample. No raw archive replay, Stockfish work, or Lichess request.
Installing Python dependencies, if necessary, is the only online setup step.

Run
Save the ZIP in ~/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop, verify its supplied SHA256, and extract
to a new folder. Run RUN_MECHANISMS.command --workers 10 --memory-gb 10.
The reply accompanying the ZIP supplies the complete paste-ready command.
An existing compatible project/package virtualenv is reused when available.
The runner checks package integrity and runs its synthetic tests before reading
the production project. caffeinate prevents idle sleep on macOS.

Restart
Rerun the same command after interruption. Verified monthly projections and
finished comparison/model fits resume. Source changes reject stale bindings.
There is a single-run lock. Original data and original IV files are never edited.
The corrected package automatically recognizes the pinned 61aea606e126 run,
reuses valid input hash records and the verified focal metadata projection,
and checks monthly joins again. Unsealed partial monthly files are not reused.
There is no need to delete the old run, package, or cache.

Resources
At most 4 concurrent month/metadata readers; DuckDB uses up to --workers threads.
Parquet projection, spill space and sequential model fits limit memory. During
models, 40% of --memory-gb is assigned to DuckDB to leave space for NumPy arrays.
NumPy allocations and OS cache mean this is a working budget, not a process
memory hard cap. A 10 GB budget is intended for a machine with at least 16 GB RAM.
Hashes are cached by path, size and modification time. Local IO may dominate.
Progress and 30-second heartbeats are printed; month stages give an ETA.

Outputs
  ~/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop/RECEIPT_MECHANISMS_REPORT_<run>.zip
  ~/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop/RECEIPT_MECHANISMS_RUN_<timestamp>.log
  ~/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop/RECEIPT_MECHANISMS_LATEST_PRIVATE.json
Working/checkpoint files remain under the project's derived/replication/
receipt_mechanisms_v100_PRIVATE/<run>/ directory.

Upload the REPORT zip for interpretation. It includes:
  READ_RESULTS.txt                 Interpretation and limitations
  comparison_estimates.csv        Primary contrasts and sensitivities
  overlap.csv, balance.csv        Retention, effective sizes, prior behavior
  feature_flow.csv, coverage.csv  Sample and ending classifications
  material_inventory.csv         Whether forced-draw data exist in this source
  secondary_dose_estimates.csv    Separate giver-cost / recipient-benefit models
  secondary_dose_summaries.json   RD correlations, numerical diagnostics
  payoff_audit.csv               Potential-payoff arithmetic checks
  receipt_cohort_audit.csv       Exact counts for each receipt-join discrepancy
  receipt_arm_patterns.csv      Counts by observed arm/material/timing flags
  secondary_status.json         Verified or blocked secondary scope by month
  summary.json                   COMPLETE, partial, or BLOCKED status

The report ZIP contains aggregates, not individual account or game records.
Private Parquet audit rows stay in the project. A partial report is exported if
a required contract fails. Do not interpret BLOCKED as a completed result.

Scope limits
Ordinary and timeout rows come from one existing new-phase-25 frame. The larger
original receipt cohort is used separately for secondary stake models. These
populations are not silently combined. Eligibility, histories, quarantine and
cooldown rules come from the reviewed parents. Author accounts __PRIVATE_VALUE_1__/__PRIVATE_VALUE_2__
are excluded from target roles, historical behavior and later outcomes through
those author-clean caches; factual chronology is preserved.

No-material comparisons run only when explicit flags are available in the
certified source. Their absence is reported rather than inferred from an
unclassified timeout. Ordinary games lack a comparable pre-disconnection board
evaluation. The package cannot identify gratitude, perceived discretion, or
a pure gift component of the IV effect from these observational comparisons.

Read ANALYSIS_PLAN.txt for the exact estimators and interpretation rules.
Read VALIDATION.txt for what was tested and what requires the production run.
